\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The twisted form counts reversed directions}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{\theta} be a \vocabk{reflection}{fdip-00PX}, and let
#{B_x} be the bilinear form of \ref{fdip-00PB}. At a vertex
fixed by #{\theta}, and at every vertex of a homogeneous
calculus after the fibres have been identified by translation,
define
##{q^\theta_x(v)=B_x(v,\theta v).}
Off these vertices no fibrewise twisted form is defined.
The form #{q^\theta_x} is quadratic. If #{2} is invertible,
the fibre splits as the sum of the #{\pm 1} eigenspaces of
#{\theta}. Those summands are orthogonal for #{B_x}. The form
#{q^\theta} equals #{q} on the #{+1} summand and equals
#{-q} on the #{-1} summand. Over an ordered field with #{q}
positive definite, the signature of #{q^\theta} is the pair of
those two ranks. On the unit-weight symmetric torus, one
coordinate reflection has signature #{(2n-1,1)} on the rank
#{2n} fibre and signature #{(n-1,1)} on the odd quotient of
\ref{fdip-00PO}. For #{n=4} that quotient has signature
#{(3,1)}. Over #{\mathbb{F}_2} the swap of one opposite pair
is unipotent and has no complementary eigenline. Over
#{\mathbb{Z}}, #{r} independent swapped pairs span a
submodule of index #{2^r}.}

\proof{
\p{The identity #{B_x(v,v)=q_x(v)} is the normalization of
\ref{fdip-00PB}, so no step divides by #{2}. For a scalar
#{a},
##{q^\theta(av)=B(av,\theta(av))=a^2 q^\theta(v).}
The polar expression is bilinear,
##{q^\theta(v+w)-q^\theta(v)-q^\theta(w)
 =B(v,\theta w)+B(w,\theta v),}
so #{q^\theta} is a quadratic form in the same sense. Since
#{\theta} is an isometric involution,
#{B(v,\theta w)=B(\theta v,w)=B(w,\theta v)}. If #{2} is
invertible, every vector splits as
##{v=\frac{v+\theta v}{2}+\frac{v-\theta v}{2}.}
If #{\theta v=\varepsilon v} and #{\theta w=\delta w} with
#{\varepsilon,\delta\in\{\pm 1\}}, then
##{B(v,w)=B(\theta v,\theta w)=\varepsilon\delta B(v,w).}
When #{\varepsilon\delta=-1}, one has #{2B(v,w)=0}, so
#{B(v,w)} vanishes. On a #{+1} vector,
#{q^\theta(v)=q(v)}. On a #{-1} vector,
#{q^\theta(v)=-q(v)}.}

\p{On the unit-weight symmetric torus, reflection in one axis
swaps one opposite pair and fixes every other direction. The
swapped pair contributes one positive line and one negative
line. The negative line is the odd vector of \ref{fdip-00PO}.
The remaining #{2n-2} directions stay positive, so the fibre
has signature #{(2n-1,1)}. The odd identification of that
lemma sends the reversed odd vector to its negative and fixes
the other odd lines, which is the signature #{(n-1,1)}.}

\p{Over #{\mathbb{F}_2} the swap of a pair has matrix
#{\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}} relative to the two
edges. That matrix equals the identity plus a matrix of square
zero, so the only eigenvalue is #{1} and there is no
complementary eigenline. Over #{\mathbb{Z}} the
same pair has index #{2} by \ref{fdip-00PO}, and #{r}
independent pairs give index #{2^r}. On the rank #{2n}
fibre the signature is #{(2n-1,1)}. For #{n=4} the quotient
has signature #{(3,1)}.}
}
